  <title>5.15.3: Growth Rate of an Exponential Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01520</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.3: Growth Rate of an Exponential Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5caa0279-1292-45ba-b30e-69a798f1a9ec</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para>Here is a table that shows some input and output values of an exponential function \(g\). The equation \(g(x)=3^x\)
  defines the function.</para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        \(x\)
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        \(g(x)\)
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>3</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>27</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>4</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>81</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>5</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>243</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>6</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>729</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>7</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2,187</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>8</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>6,561</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x+1\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How does \(g(x)\) change every time \(x\) increases by 1? Show or explain your reasoning.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The output values increase by a factor of 3. For example: When we divide two consecutive output values, the
      quotient is always 3. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Choose two new input values that are consecutive whole numbers and find their output values. Record them in
        the table. How do the output values change for those two input values?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(g(10)=3^{10}\) and \(g(11)=3^{11}\). The output still grows by a factor of 3 when the input increases by 1
      (from 10 to 11). </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Complete the table with the output when the input is \(x\) and when it is \(x+1\).
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
      <thead>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
          </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(g(x)\)
          </emphasis></entry></row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para>\(x\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>\(3^x\)</entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para>\(x+1\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>\(3^{x+1}\)</entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Look at the change in output values as the \(x\) increases by 1. Does it still agree with your earlier
        findings? Show your reasoning.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes, the outputs still increase by a factor of 3. By rules of exponents, we know that \(3x \cdot 3=3^{x+1}\). (Or
      by rules of exponents, we know that \(\frac {3^{x+1}}{3^x}=3^{x+1-x}\), which equals 3.) </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>Pause here for a class discussion. Then, work with your group on the next few questions.</para>


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Choose two \(x\)-values where one is 3 more than the other (for example, 1 and 4). How do the output values
        of \(g\) change as \(x\) increases by 3? (Each group member should choose a different pair of numbers and study
        the outputs.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The output values increase by a factor of 27 or \(3^3\). </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Complete this table with the output when the input is \(x\) and when it is \(x+3\). Look at the change in
        output values as \(x\) increases by 3. Does it agree with your group’s findings in the previous question?
        Show your reasoning. 
        <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis>
                \(x\)
              </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
                \(g(x)\)
              </emphasis></entry></row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <para>\(x\)</para>
              </entry>
              <entry/>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <para>\(x+3\)</para>
              </entry>
              <entry/>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
      <thead>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
          </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(g(x)\)
          </emphasis></entry></row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para>\(x\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>\(3^x\)</entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para>\(x+3\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>\(3^{x+3}\)</entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <para>Yes, the outputs still increase by a factor of 27. By rules of exponents, we know that \(3x \cdot 3^3=3^{x+3}\).
      (Or by rules of exponents, we know that \(\frac{3^{x+3}}{3^x}=3^{x+3-x}\), which equals \(3^3\) or 27.) </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


